# Shrinking with whichever codec here wins on the tree at hand.
#
# Only the deferred form can choose per tree, which is why there is no
# [encode_best] beside it: [Codec.shrink]'s result carries the decode it needs,
# so nothing downstream has to know which codec made it, where a payload on its
# own does not say.
#
# It costs every encoding, and the term codec's is mostly the check that its
# candidate gives the tree back, so this is for a tree whose size is worth
# paying that once at build time. The two grammar codecs share the work of
# finding the grammar, the evaluator remembering it.

shrink_best = \t List.foldl (\best \c Codec.smaller best (Codec.shrink c t)) t
  [Codec.Grammar.codec; Codec.Huffman.codec; Codec.Term.codec]

# Which one wins says something about the tree. The grammar codecs win
# wherever a tree is merely repetitive, by a wide margin on a large one: the
# Huffman one where the grammar is large enough to pay for its larger decoder,
# Codec.Grammar below that. The term codec wins where a program was written
# with it in mind — binding what its inner code uses close to it — and the
# selfie is the one such program here; its own tests keep that figure, since
# depending on it from this module would be a cycle.
_to_string = \l List.join " → " $ List.map Nat.to_string l
[size Lambada.parse_expr; size (shrink_best Lambada.parse_expr)]
# = 491776 → 4812
[size Lambada.syntax_to_native; size (shrink_best Lambada.syntax_to_native)]
# = 5007181 → 21075
# Below what any encoding costs to carry, the tree comes back as it was.
[size Bool.not; size (shrink_best Bool.not)]
# = 8 → 8

_to_string = Bool.to_source
equal (shrink_best List.map Nat.succ [1; 2]) [2; 3]
# = true
